        "id": 4269882
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-08T11:26:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for pointing this! But we are safe from race conditions here.\n\nSyncFence objects are sticky state variables, not consumable events. Once they move to a signaled state, they stay signaled forever. If a GC delays the test execution and the GPU finishes its work early (and it signals the fence), calling await() will simply see the signaled state and return true instantly (and the test passes because we are checking assertTrue(signaled).\n\nAdditionally, When we do platformSyncFence \u003d fence.toSyncFence(), the Kotlin object takes ownership of that File Descriptor.\nBecause we assigned it to a local variable (platformSyncFence), the GC knows the object is actively being used. The GC will close it.",
      "parentUuid": "80a0e888_12d73bc8",
      "revId": "43e1c0eadcf9d8b9f5826aa1a7da4bac982b1a51",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
